  "worked_solution": "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{cos}^{2}⁡x+1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{cos}^{2}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ -(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0<\\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1,\\,0<\\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nThere are no solutions to this equation for $0<\\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidates has ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14267441/assets/b69403e052136f565ececc7d6bef63bfe8e496da36dd72e5bbf2dd8d4881f9c7.png)",
